\title{Dijkstra in practice}
\date{2026-10-07T13:58:40Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Most standard libraries I am aware of do not have a priority queue
implementation capable of doing a fast #{\mathit{updateKey}}
operation.  One can simply remove an element and re-insert it, but
this takes #{\Theta(n)} since there is no way to find an element other
than scanning over the entire heap. An implementation supporting a
fast #{\mathit{updateKey}} could, for example, keep a hash table on
the side which keeps track of the location of each item within the
heap.  Given a sorted set data structure, it is also possible to keep
a sorted set of (distance, vertex) pairs. Removing the vertex with the
minimum distance is easy since it is just the first pair in the set;
updating the distance of a vertex can be done by first deleting the
(old distance, vertex) pair from the set (which can be done in #{O(\lg
n)} since the set is sorted) and then inserting (new distance,
vertex).}
